From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 03:46:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: never walk another task's userspace stack

get_stack_pointer() and get_frame_pointer() hand a blocked task's
KSTK_ESP()/KSTK_EBP() to show_stack() and dump_trace(), which then
dereference them as kernel addresses. Those macros report the task's
*userspace* stack and frame pointers, so the walk reads guest memory.

On Darwin that is fatal rather than merely wrong. An RCU CPU stall report
calls rcu_dump_cpu_stacks() -> sched_show_task() for the task running on
another CPU; show_stack() then reads its guest stack pointer, the host has
no mapping there, and the resulting SIGSEGV arrives with is_user clear, so
segv() panics with "Kernel tried to access user memory". A diagnostic that
should have described a stall kills the guest instead, and destroys the
evidence about what stalled.

Report no stack for a task that is not current. Both walkers already stop
on a NULL stack pointer - kstack_end(NULL) is true and dump_trace()
computes an empty range - so nothing that was ever meaningful is lost: the
walk previously produced guest bytes, not kernel frames.
---
 arch/um/include/asm/stacktrace.h | 16 ++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/include/asm/stacktrace.h b/arch/um/include/asm/stacktrace.h
index 436b55952c3a518dce95a9a69782cbfc8d5b4f02..1bdad33f906f5739c0559ac40e22bbf16e25057a 100644
--- a/arch/um/include/asm/stacktrace.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/asm/stacktrace.h
@@ -20,7 +20,13 @@ get_frame_pointer(struct task_struct *task, struct pt_regs *segv_regs)
 {
 	if (!task || task == current)
 		return segv_regs ? PT_REGS_BP(segv_regs) : current_bp();
-	return KSTK_EBP(task);
+	/*
+	 * KSTK_EBP() reports the task's userspace frame pointer.  Following it
+	 * would walk guest memory as if it held kernel frames, and on hosts
+	 * that do not keep an idle task's userspace mapped it faults the
+	 * kernel outright.  A blocked task has no frame pointer worth reading.
+	 */
+	return 0;
 }
 #else
 static inline unsigned long
@@ -35,7 +41,13 @@ static inline unsigned long
 {
 	if (!task || task == current)
 		return segv_regs ? (unsigned long *)PT_REGS_SP(segv_regs) : current_sp();
-	return (unsigned long *)KSTK_ESP(task);
+	/*
+	 * KSTK_ESP() likewise reports the task's userspace stack pointer, which
+	 * never addresses kernel memory.  Report no stack rather than a guest
+	 * one: both walkers stop on NULL, so a dump of another task loses
+	 * nothing that was ever meaningful.
+	 */
+	return NULL;
 }
 
 void dump_trace(struct task_struct *tsk, const struct stacktrace_ops *ops, void *data);
